  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Dry through Friday, then rain associated with\nHurricane Isaias arrives over the weekend.\n\nHigh pressure will remain over the region through Friday, with a\nslight retreat to the north and east by Friday evening. As this\nhigh pressure retreats to the northeast to be centered over New\nYork and New England by Saturday morning, surface wedging looks\nto develop across the region, with cooler northeast flow\nforecast to develop over the Piedmont, with east/southeast \nflow developing over SE West Virginia and areas predominantly\nwest of I-77 in Virginia. \n\nThis will maintain cooler temperatures over the region as\nmoisture from likely tropical depression Isaias pushes into the\nregion. Similar to winter setups with lows tracking on the west\nside of the Appalachians, the east side looks to remain cool,\nwith isentropic lift of warm moist air riding over the cooler\ndrier air at the surface. In this instance, the tropical\nmoisture will likely lead to much more impressive rainfall\ntotals over the region, with widespread 1-3 inches expected \nover the region, with localized higher totals of 2-4 inches\npossible. Favored regions for these higher totals look to be\nalong portions of the Blue Ridge as a 50-60 southeasterly 850mb\nLLJ pushes into the area as the remnants of Isaias move into\nTennessee valley. Upslope flow from this LLJ may increase local \ntotals right along the Blue Ridge. \n\nAlong with these rainfall totals, which will inevitably be\nbeneficial to the region, the aforementioned 850mb LLJ may lead\nto a downslope wind event for areas in SE West Virginia and SW\nVirginia. Right now, forecast wind gusts are just below Wind\nAdvisory criteria; however, as high res CAM guidance comes into\nrange, it is possible that these forecasted wind gusts may\nincrease across mountain locations even as whats left of Isaias\ncontinues to dissipate as it moves near the region. \n\nBy late Monday, all remnants of Isaias look to be well east of\nthe region, with dry conditions returning areawide once again. A\npossible dry frontal passage by the Tuesday night timeframe\nlooks to drop temperatures slightly through the middle of the\nwork week. Overall, the region will likely not see any rain\nchances through most of next week after the remnants from \nIsaias move through over the weekend.",
